Write each service name straight to the framebuffer

The panel stops at 'SM enter ArmCpu' and never reaches 'SM locked ArmCpu',
which is one printf later: the console path itself stops there, and everything
after it is invisible while the kernel keeps matching (run-370/371's U1 U2T U3
repeat dozens of times).

Put the first three letters of every service name on the framebuffer through
the same direct writer.  The last name visible is then the service being
started when the console stopped, which is what 'how far did it get' means.
---
diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
index fb25aa6..a2a6fd8 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
@@ -1130,6 +1130,19 @@ IOService::startMatching( IOOptionBits options )
 	bool        sync;
 	bool        waitAgain;
 
+	/* nio: say the service name straight on the framebuffer, in three letters.
+	 * The console path stops printing at some point and everything after it is
+	 * invisible, so progress has to be measured without it. */
+	{
+		const char *nio_n = getName();
+		char nio_b[8];
+		nio_b[0] = '\n'; nio_b[1] = 'B'; nio_b[2] = ' ';
+		nio_b[3] = (nio_n && nio_n[0]) ? nio_n[0] : '?';
+		nio_b[4] = (nio_n && nio_n[1]) ? nio_n[1] : ' ';
+		nio_b[5] = (nio_n && nio_n[2]) ? nio_n[2] : ' ';
+		nio_b[6] = '\0';
+		nio_u_mark(nio_b);
+	}
 	printf("\nnio: SM enter %s t=%p", getName() ? getName() : "?", current_thread());
 	lockForArbitration();
 	printf("\nnio: SM locked %s t=%p", getName() ? getName() : "?", current_thread());
